  "strategy": "**1. Approach and Rationale**\n\nThe central idea is to represent the function \\(h(x)\\) as a product of two kernel functions: one for the placement of impulses in **[0,2]** and another for their widths. This multiplicative representation reduces the non-convex optimization problem into a set of separable subproblems, often easier to optimize individually. \n\nWe believe this approach will improve upon the parent solution by capturing the interaction between impulse positions and widths in a more efficient manner, characteristic of Erd\u0151s-type problems. The bottleneck addressed is the intractability of the joint optimization, which is mitigated by relaxing it into independent optimizations.\n\n**Limitations**: 1) Assumes the optimal \\(h(x)\\) is composed of spikes (discontinuities) 2) Representations may overfit if too many parameters used. Success depends on the correct kernel separation.\n\n---\n\n**2. Mathematical Specification**\n\nSuppose we have \\(h(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^{m} a_i \\cdot \\exp(-c|x-x_i|)\\) as a basis for smooth functions (approximating discontinuous behavior) or \\(h(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^{m} a_i \\cdot (x_i \\leq x \\leq b_i)\\) for piecewise constants (better for actual discontinuities).\n\nFor optimization, we use:\n- **Position Kernel \\(p(x)\\)**: Places \\(m\\) points \\(\\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^m\\)\n- **Width Kernel \\(w_i(x)\\)**: Controls the width \\(w_i\\) of each \"spike\"\n\nWe define:\n\\[\nh(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^{m} p_i(x) \\cdot w_i(x)\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(p_i(x)\\) represents the position/magnitude component\n- \\(w_i(x)\\) controls the \"sharpness condition\"\n\nThe objective function becomes:\n\\[\n\\min_{\\theta} \\max_{k} \\int_{0}^{2} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) \\, dx\n\\]\n\nWith integral constraint:\n\\[\n\\int_{0}^{2} h(x) \\, dx = 1\n\\]\n\nVariable scaling: Let \\(\\theta\\) be the concatenated vector of parameters.\n\n---\n\n**3. Implementation Procedure**\n\n**A. Initialization (with parent fallback):**\n- If `initial_h_values` provided, project to multiplicative components:\n  - For piecewise constants: Done through adaptive clustering.\n  - For Gaussians: Fit via moment matching.\n\n**B. Core Optimization:**\n- Represent \\(h(x)\\) as a (sparse) combination of basis functions in position+width space.\n- Use coordinate descent: iteratively optimize position and width kernels.\n- Use constrained optimization with CVXPY (with box constraints).\n\n**C. Evaluation:**\n- For each candidate \\(h(x)\\):\n  - Verify constraints: \\(\\sum h_i \\Delta x = 1\\), \\(0 \\le h \\le 1\\)\n  - Compute \\(C_5(\\theta) = \\max_k \\text{integral\\_of_overlap}\\).\n\n**D. Special Cases:**\n- If no parent, start via uniform random initialization of parameters.\n- If parent provided, use its position array to initialize new parameters.\n\n---\n\n**4. Search Choices and Variations**\n\n**Initial Strategy Parameters:**\n- \\(m = 16\\): Initial number of spikes/segments.\n- Lambda schedule (regularization): Exponential decay: \\(\\lambda = \\lambda_0 \\cdot e^{-\\text{iteration}}\\)\n\n**Up-sampling Strategy:**\n- Start with coarse resolution (e.g., 256 points).\n- Upscale by adaptive factor based on functional behavior.\n\n**Hyperparameter Control:**\n- Frequencies: Use Poisson random for generating optimal placements.\n- Dependency checking: \n  - Calculate gradient per component.\n  - Rank improvement per step.\n- Robustness: \n  - Simulated annealing on temperature factor.\n  - Random restart every improvement threshold.\n\n---\n\n**5. Compute Budget and Robustness**\n\n**Phases:**\n- **Local Search**: Gradient-based optimization for 50% of budget.\n- **Global Exploration**: Population-based (e.g., evolutionary) for 30 seconds.\n- **Fallback**: From highest evaluated resolution if stuck.\n\n**Termination:**\n- Maximum 60 evaluations.\n- Decline if improvement <1e-5.\n- Time: 10s for constraint repair.\n\n---\n\n**6. Validation and Return Contract**\n\n**Post-processing:**\n- Re-calculate \\(C_5\\) to ensure variance < tolerance.\n- Check \\(\\int h(x) \\, dx == 1\\).\n- Ensure \\(h(x) \\in [0,1]\\) via projection.\n\n**Output Interface:**\n- `h_values` (array scaled to [0,1] and length n_points)\n- `c5_bound` (minimum found overlap integral)\n- `n_points` (current resolution)\n\n**Code Structure:**\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\nfrom cvxpylayers import CvxpyLayer\nfrom scipy.special import logsig\nimport time\n\ndef project_and_optimize(parent=None, n_initial=256):\n    # Define the core optimization variables\n    m = 16  # Initial number of components\n    layers = CvxpyLayer(...)  # For constraints\n    parameters = np.random(...)  # Initial guess\n\n    if parent is not None:\n        # Project parent to parameters space\n        projection = project_from_parent(parent, n_initial)\n        parameters = projection\n\n    # Formulate CVX optimality condition\n    obj = OptimalOverlap(parameters)\n    A = ObjectiveMatrix(parameters)\n    b = 1.0  # Integral constraint\n\n    for iteration in range(max_iter):\n        # Update parameters and resample\n        ...\n        # Check performance\n        violation = check_constraints(parameters)\n        if violation < tol:\n            break\n\n    return parameters, c5_value\n```\n\n---\n\nSee full code details within the core implementation sections.",
